    "markdown": "---\ntitle: Using Other Solvers\nauthor: Anqi Fu and Balasubramanian Narasimhan\ncategories: []\ntags: []\nparams:\n  mode: ignore\n  testdata_dir: test_data/1.9.3\n  data_dir: using-other-solvers\n---\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n## Introduction\n\n`CVXR` supports 15 conic and quadratic-program solvers, including several\nopen-source solvers and optional commercial ones, plus two nonlinear-programming\n(NLP) solvers for disciplined nonlinear programs.\n\n### Open Source Solvers (on CRAN)\n\n- [Clarabel](https://oxfordcontrol.github.io/clarabel-r/), a versatile\n  conic solver written in Rust (**default solver**).\n- [SCS](https://github.com/cvxgrp/scs), a conic solver using operator\n  splitting, effective for large-scale problems.\n- [OSQP](https://www.osqp.org), for quadratic and linear programs.\n- [HiGHS](https://highs.dev/), for linear, quadratic, and\n  mixed-integer linear programs.\n- [ECOS / ECOS_BB](https://github.com/embotech/ecos), an interior-point\n  solver for second-order and exponential cone programs (ECOS_BB adds\n  mixed-integer support and is used only when named).\n- [GLPK / GLPK_MI](https://www.gnu.org/software/glpk/), for linear\n  programs and mixed-integer linear programs.\n- [CVXOPT](https://cvxopt.org/), for linear, second-order cone, and\n  semidefinite programs.\n- [PIQP](https://predict-epfl.github.io/piqp/), a proximal\n  interior-point solver for quadratic programs.\n- [SCIP](https://www.scipopt.org/), for (mixed-integer) LP, QP, and\n  second-order cone programs. Free for academic and\n  non-commercial use; installed from r-universe.\n\n### Commercial Solvers\n\n- [MOSEK](https://www.mosek.com/), supporting LP, QP, SOCP, SDP,\n  exponential/power cone, and mixed-integer programs. Free academic licenses\n  available.\n- [Gurobi](https://www.gurobi.com/), supporting LP, QP, SOCP, and\n  mixed-integer programs. Free academic licenses available.\n- [CPLEX](https://www.ibm.com/products/ilog-cplex-optimization-studio),\n  supporting LP, QP, SOCP, and mixed-integer programs.\n- [FICO Xpress](https://www.fico.com/en/products/fico-xpress-optimization),\n  supporting LP, QP, SOCP, and mixed-integer programs.\n\n### Nonlinear (NLP) Solvers\n\nFor smooth nonlinear programs — see the [DNLP Tutorial](../dnlp/tutorial.qmd),\nsolved with `psolve(prob, nlp = TRUE)` — `CVXR` supports two open-source NLP\nsolvers, each via an optional R package in `Enhances` (not installed by default;\n`CVXR` checks for them at solve time and errors informatively if one is missing):\n\n- [Uno](https://cran.r-project.org/package=Uno) (the `Uno` package), Unifying Nonlinear\n  Optimization. On CRAN and self-contained (bundles its own MUMPS/HiGHS),\n  so it is the practical default for R users; it also recovers `dual_value()`.\n- [IPOPT](https://bnaras.github.io/ipopt/) (the `ipopt` package), an\n  interior-point solver. Not on CRAN owing to licensing, so installed only by\n  determined users; when present, `nlp = TRUE` prefers it (matching CVXPY).\n\n## Solver Capabilities\n\nThe table below shows each solver, its R package dependency, and the\nproblem classes it supports.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n`````{=html}\n<table class=\"table table-striped\" style=\"margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-bottom: 0;\">\n<caption>Solver Capabilities</caption>\n <thead>\n  <tr>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> Solver </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> R Package </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> LP </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> QP </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> SOCP </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> SDP </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> EXP </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> POW </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> MILP </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> MIQP </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> MISOCP </th>\n  </tr>\n </thead>\n<tbody>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !imp